WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 3.3: Forwarding and Testing DNS. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 3.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
3, Forwarding and Testing DNS.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.700
First you quickly rebuild your zones from
Labs 3.

4
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.150
1 and 3.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.400
2, and then your DNS servers learn to work
together.

6
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.650
You will see where a server sends a
question it cannot answer, and you will

7
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.300
add a conditional forwarder for branch.

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:23.750
test.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.200
You will also watch a forwarder fail,
because our pod has no internet.

10
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:33.450
Then we build a testing toolkit and a
troubleshooting flow.

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.100
Last, two short concepts from the exam:
DNSSEC and DNS policies.

12
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.750
CLIENT01 asks SRV01 for a name.

13
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.200
First, SRV01 checks its own zones, like
lakeshore.

14
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:45.650
test.

15
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.300
Next, its cache of recent answers.

16
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.750
If neither has it, SRV01 asks another
server.

17
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.200
If the name ends in a domain with a
conditional forwarder, like branch.

18
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.650
test, it asks the server you listed,
SRV-CORE.

19
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.100
For any other name it uses its forwarders.

20
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.950
If those fail, it falls back to root
hints.

21
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:14.200
In our pod only the SRV-CORE path can
work, because the internet is out of

22
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:15.650
reach.

23
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.700
A conditional forwarder covers one named
domain and lives in the Conditional

24
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.150
Forwarders node.

25
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.400
A forwarder covers every name the server
cannot answer, and you set it on the

26
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.050
Forwarders tab in the server's properties.

27
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:37.700
Root hints are the fallback: a list of the
root servers at the top of DNS.

28
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:43.950
Asking other servers for a client is
called recursion, and you can turn it off

29
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.800
on the Advanced tab.

30
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:51.650
A stub zone is an alternative: it copies
the other zone's name server records.

31
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.900
In DNS Manager, right-click Conditional
Forwarders and choose New Conditional

32
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.350
Forwarder.

33
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.600
In DNS Domain, type branch.

34
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.050
test.

35
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:07.300
Under IP addresses of the master servers,
click the line that says click here to

36
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.950
add, and type SRV-CORE's address, 192.

37
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.400
168.

38
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.850
50.

39
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.300
21.

40
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.550
DNS Manager checks the address.

41
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.000
Validated should say OK, which means
SRV-CORE answered as the owner of the

42
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.450
zone.

43
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:30.100
The option to store it in Active Directory
is greyed out, because SRV01 is not a

44
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.550
domain controller.

45
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.000
You will use it in Learning Plan 4.

46
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.450
Now in PowerShell.

47
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.100
Before the change, Resolve-DnsName for
app1.

48
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:40.550
branch.

49
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.000
test fails.

50
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:48.650
SRV01 has no zone and no forwarder for it,
and the root servers cannot be reached.

51
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.500
Then one command,
Add-DnsServerConditionalForwarderZone,

52
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.350
with dash Name branch.

53
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.600
test and dash MasterServers 192.

54
00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.050
168.

55
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.500
50.

56
00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:58.950
21.

57
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.400
The output shows a zone of type Forwarder.

58
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.250
Without Active Directory, it is stored in
the registry.

59
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:12.900
Clear the server cache so an old failure
is not reused, and then resolve the name

60
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.350
again.

61
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.200
Now SRV01 asks SRV-CORE for you and
returns 192.

62
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.650
168.

63
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.100
50.

64
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.550
21.

65
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.400
Now the general forwarders.

66
00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.250
Right-click SRV01, choose Properties, and
open the Forwarders tab.

67
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.700
The list holds the servers SRV01 asks for
every name it cannot answer.

68
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.750
You add or remove them with Edit.

69
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.400
In our pod we add 8.

70
00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:40.850
8.

71
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.300
8.

72
00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.750
8, a public resolver, and the check fails,
because there is no internet.

73
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Below the list is a check box: Use root
hints if no forwarders are available.

74
00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:57.050
On means try the root servers too.

75
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.900
And notice the note: conditional
forwarders win over server-level

76
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.350
forwarders.

77
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.400
In PowerShell, Set-DnsServerForwarder sets
the forwarder list.

78
00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.250
Set replaces the list.

79
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.100
Get-DnsServerForwarder shows the address
and the root hint setting.

80
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.950
Now ask for www.

81
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.400
microsoft.

82
00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.850
com, and wait.

83
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:21.700
You may get a timeout after several
seconds, or a quick DNS server failure.

84
00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.350
That is not a setup mistake.

85
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.200
The pod has no internet, so nobody can
answer.

86
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:29.650
Meanwhile, app1.

87
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.100
branch.

88
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.950
test still works, because the conditional
forwarder comes first.

89
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.200
When you are done, clean up with
Remove-DnsServerForwarder, because a

90
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.650
forwarder that cannot work only slows
things down.

91
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.700
nslookup is the classic DNS testing tool.

92
00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:49.150
Type nslookup alone, and you enter
interactive mode.

93
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.400
It first tells you which server it asks:
here srv01.

94
00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:54.850
lakeshore.

95
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.300
test.

96
00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.750
Type a name, and you get the answer.

97
00:04:60.000 --> 00:05:04.800
Non-authoritative answer means SRV01 got
it second-hand, from SRV-CORE or from its

98
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.250
cache.

99
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.900
Set type equals mx asks for mail records
instead of addresses.

100
00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.550
The server command switches to another DNS
server, only inside nslookup.

101
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.000
Set debug shows every detail of each
reply.

102
00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:20.850
Type exit to leave.

103
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:26.700
Resolve-DnsName is the PowerShell way to
test names, and its results are easy to

104
00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.150
filter and compare.

105
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.000
By default it asks for A and AAAA records.

106
00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:33.850
Four switches matter most.

107
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.300
Dash Type picks the record type, like MX.

108
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:41.950
Dash Server skips your network setting and
asks one server directly.

109
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:47.400
Dash NoHostsFile skips the hosts file, so
you see what DNS really says.

110
00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:52.850
Dash DnsOnly uses only the DNS protocol,
with no LLMNR or NetBIOS fallback.

111
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.500
It still reads the hosts file.

112
00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.550
First, make sure the server is reachable.

113
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.000
Test-NetConnection with dash Port 53 tries
a TCP connection to the DNS port.

114
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:09.050
TcpTestSucceeded True means the server is
up and the firewall is open.

115
00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.700
Most queries use UDP 53, but DNS servers
listen on both.

116
00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:15.150
Next, caches.

117
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:20.200
ipconfig slash displaydns shows what the
client remembers, even answers like Name

118
00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:21.650
does not exist.

119
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.500
Clear-DnsClientCache empties it, the same
as ipconfig slash flushdns.

120
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:30.150
On the server, Clear-DnsServerCache with
dash Force clears the server's cache.

121
00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.400
When a name does not resolve, follow this
flow and stop at the first failure.

122
00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.650
One: the client DNS setting.

123
00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.900
Is CLIENT01 really using 192.

124
00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.350
168.

125
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.800
50.

126
00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.250
20?

127
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.700
Two: is the server reachable on port 53?

128
00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:52.950
Three: do the zone and the record exist,
spelled correctly?

129
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.400
Four: is a forwarder set for another
domain, and does its target answer?

130
00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:02.650
Five: caches, which can hold a saved does
not exist.

131
00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:06.100
Six: a hosts file line that overrides DNS.

132
00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.750
Fix one thing, then test again.

133
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Two things can mislead you.

134
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.450
First, negative caching.

135
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:19.100
If you ask for a name before it exists,
the no travels back from SRV-CORE through

136
00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.550
SRV01 to CLIENT01, and each one remembers
it.

137
00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.400
The DNS server keeps a negative answer for
up to fifteen minutes by default.

138
00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.850
So a new record can still look missing.

139
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.100
Clear the server cache, then the client
cache, or wait.

140
00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:37.950
Second, the hosts file.

141
00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:43.800
A line there beats DNS for ping and most
apps, but nslookup ignores it.

142
00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.850
When tests are not enough, use logs.

143
00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:53.100
The Audit log is on by default and records
changes to zones, records and settings.

144
00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.150
The Analytical log is off by default.

145
00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:02.800
It records one event for each query and
answer, and it is light enough for most

146
00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:04.250
servers.

147
00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:10.100
Find it in Event Viewer under DNS-Server,
after you choose Show Analytic and Debug

148
00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.550
Logs.

149
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.200
Debug logging is the older way.

150
00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:20.050
It writes every packet to a text file, so
only turn it on briefly.

151
00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.300
DNSSEC protects answers from forgery.

152
00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:28.150
Signing a zone makes the server add a
digital signature, an RRSIG record, next

153
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.400
to each set of records.

154
00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.050
The data is signed, not encrypted.

155
00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.900
Two keys are used.

156
00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:41.150
The zone signing key signs the records,
and the key signing key signs the zone's

157
00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.600
public keys.

158
00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:48.450
A resolver that validates holds a trust
anchor, a trusted public key, and rejects

159
00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.300
answers with bad signatures.

160
00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.950
In PowerShell, Invoke-DnsServerZoneSign
signs a zone.

161
00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.400
We do not sign zones in the pod.

162
00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:00.650
DNS policies let one server give different
answers by rule.

163
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.700
A zone scope is a second set of records
inside one zone.

164
00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:11.550
A policy uses criteria, like the client
subnet, the server interface, the time of

165
00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.400
day or the record type, to choose the
scope.

166
00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:20.850
A classic use is split-brain DNS: inside
users get an internal address, outside

167
00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.100
users get a public one.

168
00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.750
The example adds an external scope, a
record and a policy.

169
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.800
Like DNSSEC, this is a concept only.

170
00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.250
Four common mistakes.

171
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.500
One: making SRV-CORE a normal forwarder
just to reach branch.

172
00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:37.950
test.

173
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.600
That sends every unknown name there.

174
00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.850
Use a conditional forwarder instead.

175
00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.900
Two: expecting forwarding to work both
ways.

176
00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:47.350
SRV01 finds branch.

177
00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.200
test, but SRV-CORE needs its own forwarder
for lakeshore.

178
00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.650
test.

179
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:55.300
Three: trusting nslookup when ping
disagrees.

180
00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:00.750
nslookup ignores the hosts file, so check
it, and use Resolve-DnsName with dash

181
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.200
NoHostsFile.

182
00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.650
Four: fixing a record while the error
stays.

183
00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:11.100
The no is cached, so clear both the client
and the server cache.

184
00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:12.950
Time to check yourself.

185
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.000
CLIENT01 uses SRV01 as its DNS server.

186
00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:17.850
First: SRV01 resolves srv01.

187
00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.300
lakeshore.

188
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.150
test but not app1.

189
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:22.600
branch.

190
00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.050
test.

191
00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.700
What do you add on SRV01?

192
00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:29.350
Second: you added the forwarder 8.

193
00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:30.800
8.

194
00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.250
8.

195
00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:33.700
8, and www.

196
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.150
microsoft.

197
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:36.600
com fails.

198
00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.250
Is the forwarder set up wrong?

199
00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.900
Third: nslookup says nas01 is 192.

200
00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:43.350
168.

201
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:44.800
50.

202
00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.850
40, but ping goes to dot 99.

203
00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.300
Why?

204
00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:55.550
And fourth: you just added a missing
record, but the client still says it does

205
00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.000
not exist.

206
00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.250
What do you do next?

207
00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.300
Pause the video and think it through.

208
00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.150
Here are the answers.

209
00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.800
One: a conditional forwarder for branch.

210
00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.050
test, with master server 192.

211
00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.500
168.

212
00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:11.950
50.

213
00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:15.000
21, in DNS Manager or with
Add-DnsServerConditionalForwarderZone.

214
00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.650
Two: no, the forwarder is fine.

215
00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:23.900
The pod has no internet, so neither the
forwarder nor the root servers can reply,

216
00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.150
and a failure is expected.

217
00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.000
Three: there is a hosts file line on
CLIENT01.

218
00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.650
ping reads it, nslookup does not.

219
00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:35.300
Four: a negative answer was cached.

220
00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.350
Clear-DnsClientCache on CLIENT01, and
Clear-DnsServerCache on SRV01.

221
00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.000
Then test the name once again.

222
00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:42.450
On SRV01, app1.

223
00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:43.900
branch.

224
00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.750
test fails at first.

225
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.800
I add a conditional forwarder for branch.

226
00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.250
test pointing at SRV-CORE, and Validated
says OK.

227
00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.300
After clearing the cache, the name
resolves.

228
00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.950
Next, I add the forwarder 8.

229
00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.400
8.

230
00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:00.850
8.

231
00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:06.300
8, and an internet name fails, just as
expected, so I remove it.

232
00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.550
On CLIENT01, nslookup shows the
non-authoritative answer and the MX

233
00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.000
record.

234
00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:18.650
Then the negative cache: I ask for reports
before it exists, add it on SRV-CORE, and

235
00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.100
it still fails until I clear both caches.

236
00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:23.550
To wrap up.

237
00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:29.400
A server answers from its own zones and
cache first, then a matching conditional

238
00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.050
forwarder, then forwarders, then root
hints.

239
00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.500
With no internet, forwarders and root
hints fail.

240
00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.750
nslookup asks one server directly, and
Resolve-DnsName adds useful switches.

241
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.800
When a name fails, follow the flow:
setting, port 53, record, forwarding,

242
00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:46.250
caches, hosts file.

243
00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.300
DNSSEC signs answers, and policies choose
answers.

244
00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:50.750
Lab 3.

245
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.600
3 practices all of this in about fifty
minutes.

246
00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.450
It is practice only.

247
00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:58.700
See you in the lab.
